## Validation cadence

After a coherent set of edits, run the narrowest typecheck/lint/tests that exercise the changed behavior. Before delivery, run the full sequence and read every exit code:

```bash
bun run typecheck > /tmp/lf-typecheck.log 2>&1; echo "typecheck EXIT=$?"; head -20 /tmp/lf-typecheck.log
bun run lint
bun run governance:check
bun run governance:check-e2e-pages
bun run governance:check-responsive
bun run governance:check-api-masking
bun run lint:pt-br:strict
```

- Do not report completion while a required command fails.
- Fix violations; do not grow an allowlist without explicit owner instruction.
- UI changes also require `design:check`, relevant E2E/build coverage and measured visual verification. For every visual implementation or visual change, agents MUST run an `impeccable` design audit and verify the rendered result with `browser-harness` before delivery; apply relevant improvements while preserving `DESIGN.md`, product intent, accessibility and responsive behavior. `impeccable` is an audit/improvement tool, not authority to override project design tokens or visual governance. If either validation cannot run, report the limitation and do not claim full visual verification.
- Database changes require the local checks in `.governance/ai/database.md`; remote application still requires authorization.
- AI-governance changes require `governance:sync`; shared skill changes require `skills:sync`. Run each sync twice and ensure the second run is idempotent.
- Critical tests require a temporary negative control that is restored exactly before completion.
- Bug fixes (debug workflow) MUST, before delivery: (1) write the regression test first (E2E in `e2e/specs/**` for UI/flow bugs, `bun test` for logic) and prove it fails on the unfixed code; (2) reproduce the bug in the rendered app with `browser-harness` and record the failing signal; (3) apply the fix and show the same test and the same `browser-harness` scenario green; (4) for UI bugs, run an `impeccable` audit. Run Playwright with `node` against a dev server built from the current tree (not a stale `next start`); a spec that was never seen failing is not a regression test. Report each step, or state which could not run.
- Never hide a command's exit code behind a pipe.

## Automated coverage

Automation replaces repeated prose only where enforcement exists. In summary:

- Typecheck validates static types, not behavior.
- ESLint validates configured TypeScript/Next/hooks rules; `any` is not blocked and complexity rules are warnings.
- Governance checks validate configured boundaries, adapters, allowlists, API masking, E2E mapping and structural responsive coverage; they do not execute behavior or inspect visual quality.
- `design:check` compares token sources only. `lint:pt-br` is a spelling heuristic.
- Tests guarantee only their executed assertions. Visual UI changes still require measured render inspection.

The complete coverage and limitations table is `.governance/ai/automated-enforcement.md`.

